# The Chihuahua build (§3.10)

Every other document in this folder validates against the **Aguascalientes**
test world: `configs/Mexico_2020/config.yaml` filtered to `MGU` code `MX01`,
11 municipios, 1,425,607 people. [README.md](README.md) is explicit about
what that buys and what it costs: Aguascalientes is "urban, industrial and
comparatively wealthy," and understates the national informality gap by
roughly half. This document is the first build against a second state —
**Chihuahua**, `MX08`, 67 municipios — run to find out whether that warning
holds up against real evidence rather than an extrapolation from national
census tables.

**The config filter is state, not fixed.** At the time of this run,
`configs/Mexico_2020/config.yaml` read:

```yaml
filter:
  level: MGU
  codes: ["MX08"]   # Chihuahua (67 municipios)
```

Every Aguascalientes figure quoted elsewhere in this folder was produced with
that same file set to `["MX01"]`. The two builds are not simultaneous outputs
of one run; they are two runs of the same pipeline against the same config
path at different filter settings. Anyone re-running either build should
check the filter before comparing new numbers against either set of figures
here.

## 1. Run

```
python3 create_world.py --config configs/Mexico_2020/config.yaml
```

Output: `output/Mexico_2020_Chihuahua/` — `world_state.h5`, `build_profile.json`
(complete, machine-readable timing/memory trace of every stage), plus two
person-level debug CSVs (`work_assignment_debug.csv`,
`commute_mode_debug.csv`) that a smaller Aguascalientes-scale run does not
need to produce separately to be readable by hand.

