## 4. Households: validated against an independent official total

`households/households.csv` — the file `source/scrape_hogcen.py` builds from
the reverse-engineered SSAS/OLAP cube documented in [the access-problem
finding](README.md#the-access-problem-is-a-finding-not-a-footnote) — is not
the only thing this pipeline holds for household counts. A companion file,
`source/totales_oficiales_municipio.csv`, carries an independently-sourced
official household total per municipio, used at build time as a check on the
joint distribution the OLAP cube reconstructs.

```
Chihuahua official total (totales_oficiales_municipio.csv, summed): 1,146,395
Chihuahua households built (this run):                              1,144,076
Coverage:                                                              99.80%
```

This is the strongest available evidence that the household composition
cube's 2020 pin is holding for at least one state: the built household count
lands within 0.2% of an independently-sourced official total, which is not
something the vintage of an undocumented endpoint would produce by accident
if it had silently drifted to a later census cycle. It does not settle the
open question raised in the access-problem finding — a household *count*
matching does not prove every household's *composition* is vintage-correct —
but it is real, positive evidence where none existed before.

## 5. Attribute coverage

Population-wide coverage (fraction of all 3,741,869 people carrying the
property), from `build_profile.json`'s final stage:

| property | coverage |
|---|---:|
| residence.household | 100.00% |
| ethnicity | 100.00% |
| cohabiting_couple | 33.16% |
| activity_status | 95.41% |
| work_mode / workplace_location | 43.12% |
| work_sector | 43.12% |
| school_location / study_mode | 26.39% |

**`ethnicity`'s 100% is an assignment coverage figure, not an export one.**
This run predates two fixes to `serialization_config.yaml` documented in
[README.md](README.md)'s "Engine foot-guns" section: every person here was
correctly assigned an ethnicity, but `population.properties` did not name
`ethnicity`, so `world_state.h5` for this specific build does not carry it.
Anything reading `ethnicity` from this run's `.h5` file will find it absent
despite this table showing full coverage — the two facts are both true and
describe different stages of the pipeline.

The venue side of this run has the same gap, for the same reason. `hospital`,
`clinic`, `residency` and `social_assistance` venues in this run's
`world_state.h5` carry their identity, geography and coordinates — S3's raw
venue counts for these four types are real and correctly loaded — but no
`industry_code` or `sizeband`, because `serialization_config.yaml` had no
`types:` block for them at build time either. Anyone querying this
specific `world_state.h5` for, say, orphanages versus elder-care residencies
will find every `residency` venue identical on every exported property. Both
gaps are fixed in the current config; neither is fixed in this run's output.

**A third, more serious issue affects this run's data itself, not just its
export.** `company_offices.yaml` had no `exclude_subset_keys`, and the engine
bug it was masking (see [README.md](README.md)'s "Engine foot-guns", seventh
entry) means every `student_employee` who got a part-time job at a company
with full-time staff had their `primary_activity` silently overwritten with
that company's office. Checked directly against this run's `world_state.h5`:
of 69,238 `student_employee` people, 69,061 (99.7%) have `primary_activity`
pointing at an `office` rather than a school, university, or classroom. This
is not an export gap like the two above — the underlying simulated placement
is wrong, not merely unexported, and it is **not** fixable by re-reading this
`world_state.h5` differently. It requires a rerun with the fixed engine and
config.

`activity_status` at 95.41% (not 100%) is the drop-unspecified mechanism
working as designed — the same [no-invented-values
commitment](README.md#no-invented-values-one-commitment-stated-once)
documented for the national build, operating here on Chihuahua's own share of
`CONACT`/`ASISTEN` non-answers. None of Chihuahua's 67 municipios are among
the five the sources disagree about (README.md's cross-source table), so this
gap is ordinary person-level dropping, not a municipio going dark the way
Seybaplaya or Honduras de la Sierra do.

`work_sector`/`workplace_location` and `school_location`/`study_mode` sit
well under 100% because they are conditional: only people `activity_status`
places into a working or studying state receive them. These are not
placement-failure rates — see S6 for the actual company-placement rate,
which is a different, smaller, eligible-conditional number.

## 6. Company placement: the headline number needs decomposing before it means anything

`work_assignment_debug.csv` — 1,624,757 rows, one per person carrying
`work_sector` — gives a diagnostic the Aguascalientes-scale docs never
needed a dedicated file for. The naive top-line number:

```
Workers with work_sector          1,624,757
  already placed in a specific venue (school/university/residency/
  social_assistance, before reaching company_distributor)     29,851
  no fixed desk (work_mode = From_Home)                      141,385
  company-eligible                                          1,453,521

Placed in a company        947,744 / 1,453,521   (65.2%)
Unplaced                    505,777 / 1,453,521   (34.8%)
```

**Correction: these raw numbers are themselves contaminated by the
`company_offices` bug documented in [README.md](README.md)'s "Engine
foot-guns," seventh entry, and are wrong as published.**
`export_work_assignment_debug` (`may/utils/debug_output.py`) determines
`placed_in_company` by reading each person's `primary_activity` and checking
whether it resolves to an `office` venue with subset `"worker"`. That is
exactly the field the bug corrupted: every `student_employee` whose school
placement was overwritten with an office now reads as `placed_in_company:
True` in this diagnostic, regardless of what their real primary activity
was. Checked directly: of the 1,453,521 company-eligible rows, 63,667 are
`student_employee` people, and 63,512 of those were marked placed —
inflating the placed count and understating the true shortfall. Corrected
by excluding `student_employee` PersonIDs (identified from
`population/properties/activity_status`) from the placed count entirely,
since none of them belong in a company-placement denominator to begin with
(their real workplace question, if any, is `part_time_work`, which this
diagnostic does not measure):

```
Placed in a company (corrected)    884,232 / 1,453,521   (60.8%)
Unplaced (corrected)               569,289 / 1,453,521   (39.2%)
```

The sector breakdown below uses the corrected figures throughout, not the
raw ones — `work_assignment_debug.csv` carries `work_sector` per row, so
the same PersonID-level correction applies cell by cell:

