**Sector 62 at 100.0% (corrected) is not evidence of a coverage collapse —
it is a documented, deliberate gap, now visible cleanly instead of masked by
a second bug.** The raw figure (95.0%, 2,750 apparently placed) turned out to
be entirely an artifact: every one of those 2,750 was a misclassified
`student_employee`, not — as an earlier, uncorrected draft of this document
speculated in conversation but never verified in writing — establishments
surviving from the four excluded 621xxx auxiliary codes. With the correction
applied, *zero* sector-62 workers are placed in a company, which is exactly
what the mechanism below predicts. Sector-62 workers are meant to be placed
by `residency_distributor.yaml` and `social_assistance_distributor.yaml`,
which run before `company_distributor` and correctly claim the 623xxx/624xxx
slice of the sector before it ever reaches this diagnostic's "eligible"
pool. What is left over is the 621xxx/622xxx slice — clinic and hospital
staff — and the code says plainly why they show up as unplaced.
`company_distributor.yaml` had its own "62" matching rule removed to avoid
double-counting DENUE establishments already split out into
`hospitals.csv`/`clinics.csv`, and `residency_distributor.yaml` carries the
consequence in its own comment, verbatim:

> REMAINING GAP: that removal means sector-62 workers who work at a hospital
> (622xxx) or clinic (621xxx) currently have no employer distributor at all
> — only the 623xxx (residencia, here) and 624xxx (social assistance) slices
> are covered. They are correctly reported as unplaced until equivalent
> distributors are built for hospital/clinic workplaces, mirroring this file.

This is not new information the Chihuahua build discovered — it is a gap the
codebase already names and dates from before this run. What the Chihuahua
build supplies that the Aguascalientes-scale docs do not is the **first
measured size of it**: 55,387 people, 100% of Chihuahua's sector-62
workforce once the office-bug misclassification is removed, structurally
unplaceable by the current distributor set. `hospitals.md` S5 and
`health_social_venues.md` S7 now both carry this measurement in their own
Limitations sections — previously both discussed `employee_count`'s meaning
and the deliberate `capacity_handling: ignore` choice for *patients* without
ever stating that hospital and clinic *staff* have no workplace distributor
at all. That was a documentation gap this build log surfaced, closed in both
files rather than left for a future reader to rediscover.

Sector 61 (64.4% unplaced, corrected) is a smaller version of the same
shape, but partially real rather than purely diagnostic:
`specific_workplace_classrooms_distributor.yaml`'s own fallback note says a
teacher who finds no classroom in their workplace municipio "falls through
to company_distributor, which still matches sector 61 against DENUE
servicios educativos establishments" — a much smaller pool than actual
schools. The 32,263 sector-61 people counted unplaced here already failed
*both* steps, so unlike sector 62 this is a genuine, if partial, placement
failure rather than a diagnostic artifact.

**Recomputing the headline rate with sectors 61 and 62 set aside** — since
neither belongs in a company-placement denominator by design —:

```
eligible' = 1,453,521 - 50,099 - 55,387 =  1,348,035
placed'   =   884,232 -  17,836 -      0 =    866,396   (corrected)
unplaced' = eligible' - placed'          =    481,639   (35.7%)
```

35.7% is well above Aguascalientes' *observed* 12.5% and above the 21.3%
national *prediction* — construction (77.6%), mining (81.9%), sector 61
(64.4%, structurally different from the other three — see above), and
agriculture (46.1%) are the largest contributors, and construction,
mining and agriculture are sectors [companies.md](companies.md) already
documents as structurally under-registered in DENUE because the work does
not happen at a fixed, addressable premises. Chihuahua's economy leans
further into exactly those sectors than Aguascalientes' does.

**The reconciliation this section originally deferred has since been run,
in [README.md](README.md)'s Scope section, and the result is itself the
more interesting finding — more so once the correction above is factored
in.** The same (municipio × sector) predicted-shortfall calculation
[companies.md](companies.md) S4 runs for Aguascalientes, rerun specifically
against Chihuahua's own capacity table, predicts a company-placement
shortfall of **15.5%** — not 35.7%. Aguascalientes' predicted 12.8% and its
build's observed 12.5% agree almost exactly; at Chihuahua's scale, predicted
and observed pull apart by more than a factor of two. Part of the gap is
explained: `companies.csv` still carries 78,025 of sector-62
employee-capacity for Chihuahua that no live distributor can route anyone
to, for the exact reason S6 above documents — zeroing it lifts the
prediction to 19.3%, still well short of 35.7%. This gap is *wider* than it
first appeared: an earlier version of this comparison used the uncorrected
31.5% observed figure, understating it.

**Resolved: 35.7% was itself measuring the wrong denominator, not a real gap
against the prediction.** `export_work_assignment_debug`
(`may/utils/debug_output.py`) flagged `company_eligible` for anyone holding a
`work_sector`, filtering out only people already placed in a specific venue
and `From_Home`/`Other` work-mode people — with no check on
`activity_status`. But `company_distributor.yaml`'s own `global_filters`
require `activity_status == "employee"`: `work_sector_assignment.yaml`
assigns a sector to `["employee", "own_account", "student_employee",
"student_own_account"]` alike, so `own_account` and `student_own_account`
people carry a `work_sector` despite never being candidates for a company
placement — company_distributor never considers them, because it filters on
`activity_status` before it filters on anything else. The 63,667
`student_employee` case above was one instance of this same class of error;
this section's own correction removed them from the *numerator* (so their
false placements stopped counting as successes) but left them, and every
`own_account`/`student_own_account` person, in the *denominator* — still
counted as eligible, still counted as unplaced, despite never having been
eligible for company placement in the first place.

Measured directly against this run's `world_state.h5` — real `activity_status`
per person, real placement via actual company/office subset membership, not
approximation — of the 1,479,123 people holding a `work_sector` in sectors
other than 61/62, 355,678 (24%) do not have `activity_status == "employee"`.
Restricting to `activity_status == "employee"` only:

```
eligible (employee only, sectors 61/62 excluded)   1,084,762
placed                                               946,759
unplaced                                             138,003   (12.7%)
```

**12.7%, not 35.7%.** That lands almost exactly on the capacity-based
prediction once the prediction itself is corrected the same way this run's
capacity was undercounted: the 15.5%/19.3% figures above were computed
against `companies.csv` alone, but `venues_config.yaml` loads the `company`
venue type from `companies.csv` **and** `companies_agriculture.csv` stacked
together (a synthetic supplement — see its own `agro_<geo>_<n>` naming — that
DENUE structurally excludes; see `company_distributor.yaml`'s fallback
comment). Recomputing the (municipio × sector) prediction with that capacity
included, sectors 61/62 excluded, gives **12.0%** for Chihuahua — 0.7
percentage points from the 12.7% actually observed once the denominator is
fixed. The two independent routes to this number — a capacity-side
recalculation using only the source CSVs, and a demand-side recount using
only the completed build's own `world_state.h5` — agree with each other
almost exactly, which is the strongest evidence available that neither is a
coincidence.

The `debug_output.py` fix (`activity_status == "employee"` added to
`company_eligible`, mirroring `company_distributor.yaml`'s own filter) means
a fresh run of this diagnostic will report the corrected figure directly;
`work_assignment_debug.csv` also now carries an `activity_status` column so
this class of denominator error is visible in the raw data itself, not only
in a hand-reconciliation like this one. The 15.5%/19.3% predictions above are
themselves superseded by the companies_agriculture.csv-inclusive recompute
described here and should not be read as the last word on the capacity side
either — they are left in place above as the historical record of how this
section's reasoning developed.

## 7. What this run does not cover

- **Commute mode.** `commute_mode_debug.csv` has one row per person
  (2,474,712) but every `commute_mode` value is empty — expected, since
  [README.md](README.md) already documents `commute_mode_assignment` as not
  built for Mexico (`MED_TRASLADO_TRAB1..3` is the intended source and is not
  wired in here either). `commute_mode_debug_summary.txt` is consequently
  empty; this is the diagnostic correctly having nothing to summarize, not a
  broken script.
- **School and university placement rates.** Unlike work assignment, no
  person-level debug CSV was produced for `school_distributor`/
  `university_distributor` in this run, so this build cannot reproduce
  [schools.md](schools.md) S4's eligible-vs-placed accounting (99.6% school,
  80.1% university in Aguascalientes) for Chihuahua. The population-wide
  `school_location` coverage figure in S5 (26.39%) is not a substitute for
  that — it is a coverage-of-population rate, not an eligible-conditional
  placement rate, and the two are not comparable.
- ~~A closed predicted-vs-observed reconciliation~~ — **resolved, see S6's
  second update.** The 35.7%/19.3% gap was not a modelling shortfall: 24% of
  the denominator behind 35.7% were `own_account`/`student_own_account`
  people who were never `company_distributor`-eligible, and the 15.5%/19.3%
  predictions themselves undercounted capacity by omitting
  `companies_agriculture.csv`. Corrected on both sides, observed (12.7%) and
  predicted (12.0%) agree to within a point.
